*/ -}}
**This session was started ahead of need. Read in, then wait — do not start the work.**

Nobody has asked for E-{{.spawned_id}} yet. You were started now so that when the user is ready, they resume a session that already has the plan and the code loaded and has already asked its questions. Until then: do not claim the task, do not change code, and do not ask the user anything in chat — they are not here.

1. Run `endless guide`, then `endless task show E-{{.spawned_id}} --all-fields --db main`, and read the code the task touches.
{{- if .drafting}}
2. The task has no plan. Draft one: write it under `.endless/tmp/` and attach it with `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --plan-file <path> --db main`. An adversarial challenge reads it against the task before it is attached; if it refuses, revise and attach again. Leave every decision only the user can make out of the plan — that is step 3.
{{- else if .framing_suffices}}
2. This is a {{.task_type}}: its framing is the starting point, and a plan is not expected yet. Read it until you understand what is being asked and what is missing from it.
{{- else}}
2. Judge whether the plan is sufficient to implement from as written: every change concrete, every decision made, a way to tell it is done.
{{- end}}
3. Write every question you cannot answer from the plan or the code to the task, as one series: `endless question ask E-{{.spawned_id}} "<question>" ["<question>" …] --db main`. Do not guess an answer to make a question go away. Each one waits for the user, and the task cannot be claimed until it is answered.
4. Run `endless session primed`, then end your turn with a few lines: what you read, what you drafted if anything, and what you asked.

When the user resumes you, a note will say so and check whether the code the plan cites has moved. Then claim the task (`endless task claim E-{{.spawned_id}} --db main`) and follow the instructions below.

